in Dark Souls 2 you lose a little bit of  your humanity and a little bit of your  maximum health with each death  eventually you overcome the obstacle  that stood between you and victory but  your game does not come without  sacrifice you have forfeited your own  humanity as you collect the souls of the  Damned like Dark Souls and Demon's Souls  before it Dark Souls 2 is not just a  fantasy role-playing adventure but a  phenomenal exploration of dread few  games make you this afraid to peer  around the corner while simultaneously  curious as to what incredible new Vista  you might gaze upon and death is so very  beautiful in this game for it comes at  the hands of hulking armored Knights and  otherworldly invaders welcome to  drangleic a world that makes you ask  many questions and then expects you to  go seeking the answers Dark Souls 2  offers you an early taste of hope  however in the hub area known as medulla  while medulla is mostly devoid of life  when you first come upon it it slowly  fills out with the vendors you meet upon  your travels your most important contact  there is the cloaked woman who levels  you up in exchange for souls the game's  currency as with its predecessors there  are no waypoints and there is no quest  log instead you simply head out into  drangleic seeking to light primal  bonfires and thus restore some dignity  to this decrepit land the basics are  very similar to those of Demon Souls and  Dark Souls you defeat enemies in brutal  and precise combat collect their souls  and spend those souls on levels  equipment and more if you die you lose  the souls you were carrying and have one  chance to retrieve them should you die  again those souls are gone some of the  differences between Dark Souls and Dark  Souls 2 are noticeable early on for  instance  usually when you die or rest at a  bonfire the world is refreshed and the  standard enemies you killed respawn but  in Dark Souls 2 there comes a point at  which many enemies in the area just  don't respawn  on anymore allowing you freedom to  progress with fewer obstacles in yourway  at first you might worry that this  change and others might soften the  game's edge but the grinding  opportunities are still there  Dark Souls two's main hook though is the  promise of new and exciting places to  explore and new and exciting foes to  face and that hook is supported by a  number of subtle changes to the formula  for example once you activate a bonfire  you can warp to it from any other  bonfire without having to pass through  perilous places over and over again and  the excitement of finding an item that  in turn unlocks a new area is a far  greater thrill than grinding for Souls  the early forests and ruins are so very  dark souls but the intricate  architecture and carefully planned enemy  locations make even familiar looking  environments fresh and unique the more  progress you make however the more  unusual and awesome the settings become  though the lighting is somewhat flat the  settings are often incredible Dragons  soar in the skies castles loom in the  background and glowing sunsets give you  the slightest smidgen of hope and so  Dark Souls 2 is hard but is it harder  than the original not quite nor are the  bosses as awesome to look at as SIF or  the gaping dragon but the bosses are  still visually inventive and the game  offers plentiful challenge trudging your  way through shin deep water while  avoiding homing missiles is no easy task  just as before help comes from other  players as well as from the game itself  all Dark Souls 2 players are intertwined  in a comforting web of communication as  before you see the spirits of other  players as they journey through their  own worlds and if you feel truly  cooperative you can avail yourself to  other players who can summon you in for  assistance you can also hinder other  players by invading their worlds just as  before though certain additions to  online play keep battles more dynamic  than ever for instance if you use a  particular item monsters turn on your  enemies luring an invader into a trap is  an  absolute delight there's so so so much  more to talk about with Dark Souls 2  there's the fantastic stretch near the  end of the game that involves you in  grander battles then you would expect  from this series then there are the  covenants fellowships that bond you with  other players and give you more tools to  assist or annihilate each other how some  of these covenants may change the very  feel of the game is still unclear at  this early stage but there's one thing  you can count on Dark Souls 2 is a big  fantastic and satisfying game and a  worthy successor to one of the best RPGs  ever made  you
 

